ould it do? \"Track and validate the metering data schema against reporting requirements; confirm the reporting data contract with RHAISTRAT-9990.\" Signals: question_specificity +1 (concrete: does the schema provide per-project GPU-hours queryable by time range?); source_accessibility 0 (schema not final — answer doesn't exist yet, in-flight); local_runnability 0 or 1; cluster_hardware_dependence -1?; human_judgment_required -2 (the gating question is cross-team coordination the AI can't decide — schema owned by another strategy). That routes Low → assign to a person. Hmm, that seems... actually realistic! Cross-strategy contract validation IS a human/coordination-heavy investigation.\n\nBut wait — is an Investigation the right treatment, or is this purely Step 5.5 tiered delivery? The prompt's Step 5.5 exists exactly for \"external dependency with uncertain timing.\" The dependency here: metering data schema from RHAISTRAT-9990. Rule 18: \"External dependency with uncertain timing → always evaluate for tiered delivery (see Step 5.5).\"\n\nI think BOTH apply complementarily:\n- Tiered delivery: split quota-side (Tier 1, independent) from metering-consuming reporting (Tier 2).\n- The Tier 2 epics are gated on the schema. How to gate? The schema isn't an epic in this strategy. Options: (i) an Investigation epic \"validate/consume the metering data contract\" that blocks Tier 2 report implementation; (ii) model as precondition with AC gates, no epic.\n\nRule 21 analog: \"If third party resolves → model as precondition with tiered delivery, no separate epic.\" The third party here is RHAISTRAT-9990 (in-flight, separate strategy, will land on its own) — analogous to \"third party resolves.\" So: model as precondition + tiered delivery, NO separate epic for the dependency itself. But an Investigation to define/validate the reporting data contract could still be legitimate Tier 1 risk-reduction...\n\nHmm, hmm. Let me weigh the two candidate shapes:\n\n**Shape A (no Invest