{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a0cfbab9e5a99bc2","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9018-E002\n3\ttitle: GPU chargeback report computation, CSV export, and reporting API\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9018\n5\tcomponent: Dashboard\n6\tteam: RHOAI Dashboard\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies: []\n10\tai_signals:\n11\t  change_specificity: 0\n12\t  pattern_precedent: 0\n13\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n14\t  existing_foundation: 1\n15\t  open_questions: -1\n16\t  external_dependency: -1\n17\t  human_process_gates: 0\n18\t  repo_access: 1\n19\t  architecture_claims: -1\n20\timplementation_type: null\n21\tinvestigation_signals: null\n22\tai_implementability: Low\n23\tai_implementability_score: -1\n24\tjira_key: null\n25\tbranch: null\n26\tgated_by: null\n27\tgate_failure_impact: null\n28\t---\n29\t# GPU chargeback report computation, CSV export, and reporting API\n30\t\n31\t## Description\n32\t\n33\tThis epic delivers the backend of administrator chargeback reporting: computation of per-project GPU-hour consumption reports over selectable time ranges from the metering data delivered by the GPU usage metering strategy (RHAISTRAT-9990), plus export of those reports as CSV and retrieval via a stable API for finance tooling. It consumes the metering data; it must not re-implement collection.\n34\t\n35\t## Scope\n36\t\n37\t- Compute per-project GPU-hour consumption reports over administrator-selectable time ranges, sourced from the metering data delivered by the GPU usage metering strategy.\n38\t- Export the computed reports as CSV.\n39\t- Expose the computed reports through a stable API suitable for finance tooling integration, with a documented API contract.\n40\t- Expose the reporting capability behind the Dashboard's existing per-component feature-flag mechanism so it can be enabled/disabled per deployment.\n41\t- Resolve the hosting placement of the reporting API and computation against the Dashboard's architectural constraint that it exposes no private component APIs — the placement decision belongs to the implementing team and must be recorded.\n42\t\n43\t## Acceptance Criteria\n44\t\n45\t- Per-project GPU-hour consumption reports are computable over selectable time ranges and return correct per-project totals for the selected range.\n46\t- Reports are computed exclusively from the metering data delivered by the GPU usage metering strategy; no metering collection logic is re-implemented (verified by review: no collection path added).\n47\t- Reports are exportable as CSV with per-project GPU-hour totals for the selected time range.\n48\t- Reports are retrievable via the stable API, and the API contract (endpoints, request/response semantics, stability guarantees) is documented for finance tooling integration.\n49\t- The reporting capability can be enabled and disabled via the existing feature-flag mechanism.\n50\t- Existing Dashboard build pipelines remain green with the changes.\n51\t\n52\t## Risks / Notes\n53\t\n54\t- External timing risk: the metering data schema finalizes only when RHAISTRAT-9990 lands. This epic is ready to execute against the schema contract and is scheduled behind that precondition; if the metering strategy slips, this epic (and the administrator view built on it) is deferred rather than reworked.\n55\t- The metering data schema is owned by RHAISTRAT-9990; any gap between the schema and the reporting needs (e.g., per-project breakdown or time-range queryability) must be fed back to that strategy rather than worked around with local collection.\n56\t- Architecture note: the Dashboard architecture context states Kubernetes-API-only interfaces and no private component APIs; the placement of the stable finance-facing API must be resolved consistently with that constraint.\n57\t\n58\t## HLR Traceability\n59\t\n60\t- HLR-1 (P0) — computation side: administrators can view per-project GPU-hour consumption reports over selectable time ranges, computed from the metering data delivered by RHAISTRAT-9990.\n61\t- HLR-2 (P1, bundled) — reports can be exported as CSV and retrieved via a stable API for finance tooling. Bundled with HLR-1's computation because CSV export and the finance API serialize the same computed report data over the same aggregation; a separate epic would rebuild the aggregation surface and add an artificial serialization boundary.\n62\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b2091d34576a6f9e","agent_id":"aa99bb01dbbce016e","session_id":"2d213be9-f60a-4255-a607-da491d2b37a9","uuid":"32d3a08d-24de-46f8-a594-e95b3d672719","timestamp":"2026-10-26T17:25:56.638Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a0cfbab9e5a99bc2","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9018 decomposition"}
