{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-88f60502e66ef520","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-9018\n3\tscore: 14\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues: []\n7\terror: null\n8\t---\n9\t## Review Summary\n10\t\n11\tScore: 14/14 — pass\n12\tRecommendation: accept\n13\t\n14\tContract validator: OK (`validate_decomposition.py` exited 0 — body sections, canonical component names, Rule 11 docs-last coverage, priority inheritance, implementation-type parallelism, and shared-surface enforcement all pass).\n15\t\n16\tTriage check: `triage: null` (full decomposition), `epic_count: 4` — multi-epic output is expected; no contradiction.\n17\t\n18\tThe decomposition is a clean translation of the strategy. All four HLRs are traced to epics with bundling decisions recorded inline in the matrix rows; the DAG diagram and epic frontmatter `dependencies` match exactly; the external RHAISTRAT-9990 metering-schema dependency is correctly modeled as an AC gate plus risk (third party resolves it, no DAG node exists for it) with tiered delivery preserving independently schedulable work; and the two candidate \"investigation\" questions (schema timing, API hosting placement) are explicitly and correctly justified as non-Investigations in Health Warnings.\n19\t\n20\t## Criterion Details\n21\t\n22\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n23\tAll HLRs mapped: HLR-1 → E002/E003 (split along backend/frontend axis, both P0); HLR-2 → E002; HLR-3 → E001 (data side) + E003 (view side); HLR-4 → E004. Priority inheritance is correct: E001 maps only to HLR-3 (P1) but blocks P0 E003, so its raise to P0 is rule-mandated and explicitly explained (Health Warning 5). E004 (docs-authoring) correctly derives P1 from the strategy's `priority: Major` via the docs-authoring exception. The two lower-priority bundles (HLR-2 into E002, HLR-3 view-side into E003) are compliant, not priority collapse: each matrix row carries a `(bundled)` flag plus inline rationale (same aggregation surface / same UI surface), which is the required recording shape. HLR-2's CSV/API is serialization of the same computed report data — the recorded rationale that a separate epic would rebuild the aggregation surface is defensible.\n24\t\n25\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n26\tNo cycles; all edges reference existing epics. Cross-check passed: diagram edges (E001→E003, E002→E003, E001→E004, E002→E004, E003→E004) exactly match frontmatter `dependencies` (E003: [E001, E002]; E004: [E001, E002, E003]), and arrows run dependency → dependent per convention. Each edge is rule-justified: E001→E003 (quota data contract, Rule 13), E002→E003 (reporting API artifact, Rule 12), all implementations → E004 (Rule 11). E001 ∥ E002 correctly run parallel (different components/repos, no shared artifacts). Critical path length 3 is appropriate for a strategy of this size. The external RHAISTRAT-9990 schema dependency is correctly NOT a DAG edge (it is another in-flight strategy, not an epic in this set) — it is modeled as AC gates, risks, and tiered delivery, which is the rule-compliant shape for a third-party-resolved dependency. No data/artifact dependency discoverable from epic content is missing from the DAG: E003's consumption of both E001's contract and E002's API is captured, and E003 explicitly adds no parallel data plumbing.\n27\t\n28\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n29\tEach component/team tuple is a separate epic (Distributed Workloads/Distributed Workloads; Dashboard/RHOAI Dashboard ×2; Documentation/RHOAI Dashboard for docs-authoring). No scope duplication: E002 (backend computation/export/API) and E003 (frontend view) are distinct surfaces on different sides of the API contract, and E003 renders exclusively through E002's reporting API and E001's quota contract rather than rebuilding either. No epic exceeds ~2 weeks: E002 (computation + CSV + API + feature flag) and E003 (one administrator view with a quota panel) are each one logical change of sprint-scale. Work-product types are not mixed — content authoring is isolated in E004.\n30\t\n31\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n32\tAll four epics are Implementation and each produces a concrete artifact (quota data contract; report computation/API; admin view; published documentation). No `gated_by`/`gate_failure_impact` metadata exists to check (all null, no Investigation epics). The two questions that could have been misclassified as Investigations are correctly handled: the metering-schema timing is resolved by an external strategy (cannot be resolved by work here, and the reporting epics exist regardless — only the input contract varies), and the API hosting placement changes where code lands, not which epics exist. Both reasonings are recorded in Health Warnings 2 and 3.\n33\t\n34\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n35\tType-appropriate signal fields present on all epics (`ai_signals` for Implementation; no epic misclassified). Signal values are consistent with rationales and calibrated against `.context`: E001's `architecture_claims: 1` and `pattern_precedent: 1` are corroborated by `architecture/rhoai-3.0/distributed-workloads.md` (ClusterQueue/LocalQueue quota model, Kueue metrics exposed to Prometheus — verified present in the file); E002's `architecture_claims: -1` correctly reflects the genuine tension between HLR-2's stable finance API and `architecture/rhoai-3.0/dashboard.md`'s \"Kubernetes API only; no private component APIs\" / \"owns no data plane\" (verified verbatim in the file); E004's `architecture_claims: 0` rationale (\"no architecture-context file exists for the Documentation component\") is accurate — only dashboard.md and distributed-workloads.md exist under rhoai-3.0. E002's `open_questions: -1` and `external_dependency: -1` match the strategy's open question 1. E004's `human_process_gates: -1` matches its technical-review AC. No arithmetic or threshold checks performed (pipeline-computed).\n36\t\n37\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n38\tEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the HLRs. Rule-mandated ACs present: E002 has the feature-flag AC (existing per-component mechanism); E004 has the technical-review-with-sign-off AC; build-pipeline-green ACs on all implementation epics. No soft ACs: the external metering timing risk is correctly filed under E002's Risks/Notes (and transitively noted on E003) rather than as an AC, and E002's \"computed exclusively from the metering data / no re-implementation\" AC is a condition on the epic's own deliverable, which is valid. No draft-ID references in epic bodies (frontmatter `dependencies` lists are the only ID references, as required).\n39\t\n40\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n41\tAll strategy scope is covered: per-project reports over selectable ranges (E002/E003), CSV export and stable finance API (E002), current-month consumption vs Kueue quota (E001 data side + E003 view side, including admin-only RBAC), documentation of semantics and export integration (E004). Out-of-scope constraints are respected and enforced by AC (no collection re-implementation; reports in GPU-hours, no currency modeling). Strategy-level context is carried forward: open question 1 lives in E002/E003 risks and Health Warnings, and the missing strategy sections (no per-component change description, teams table, GWT ACs, NFRs) are explicitly flagged in Health Warning 1 rather than silently dropped. The docs-authoring epic is present for HLR-4 (the strategy has no Prerequisites & Process Gates section, but HLR-4 mandates documentation and Rule 11 coverage is satisfied).\n42\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-836a73bf9feb0d1a","agent_id":"a3028f44fb113077f","session_id":"2d213be9-f60a-4255-a607-da491d2b37a9","uuid":"039ba05b-fc29-4d0c-8b3d-74b80a7f3338","timestamp":"2026-10-26T17:28:01.400Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-88f60502e66ef520","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9018 decomposition"}
