{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a233089bb8eb4c91","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9018\n3\tepic_count: 4\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9018 (GPU chargeback reporting for platform administrators)\n10\t\n11\t## Epic List\n12\t\n13\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n14\t|-------|-------|-------|-------|----------|\n15\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E001 | Per-project Kueue quota data exposure for chargeback reporting | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P0 |\n16\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E002 | GPU chargeback report computation, CSV export, and reporting API | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P0 |\n17\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E003 | Administrator chargeback report and quota comparison view | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P0 |\n18\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E004 | Chargeback report semantics and export integration documentation | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n19\t\n20\tNotes on priorities:\n21\t- E001 maps directly only to HLR-3 (P1) but is raised to P0 by priority inheritance: it is a prerequisite of E003, which delivers the P0 admin-facing reporting (HLR-1). It is not a priority inversion — see Health Warnings.\n22\t- E004 (docs-authoring) derives P1 from the strategy frontmatter `priority: Major` per the docs-authoring priority exception; it does not push priority upstream.\n23\t\n24\t## Dependency DAG\n25\t\n26\t```mermaid\n27\tgraph TD\n28\t    E001[\"E001 Distributed Workloads: per-project Kueue quota data exposure\"] --> E003[\"E003 Dashboard: administrator report and quota comparison view\"]\n29\t    E002[\"E002 Dashboard: report computation, CSV export, reporting API\"] --> E003\n30\t    E001 --> E004[\"E004 Documentation: report semantics and export integration\"]\n31\t    E002 --> E004\n32\t    E003 --> E004\n33\t```\n34\t\n35\tExternal precondition (not a DAG node): the metering data schema owned by RHAISTRAT-9990 (in flight) gates E002 and, through it, E003. It is expressed as acceptance-criterion gates and risks on those epics, not as a dependency edge, because it is not work owned by this strategy — see Tiered Delivery.\n36\t\n37\t## DAG Justification\n38\t\n39\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n40\t|------|------|-----------|\n41\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 13 | E001 produces the per-project quota data contract that the admin view's code builds against; the view cannot be implemented without it. |\n42\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 12 | E002 produces the report/export API artifact that the admin view consumes; the frontend builds against it. |\n43\t| E001 → E004, E002 → E004, E003 → E004 | Rule 11 | docs-authoring epic is blocked by every Implementation epic in the strategy; docs describe what was built. |\n44\t| E001 ∥ E002 (no edge) | Rule 14 | Different components and repos (Distributed Workloads midstream vs Dashboard) with no shared artifacts; quota exposure and metering-data report computation are independent and must be schedulable in parallel. |\n45\t| RHAISTRAT-9990 schema → E002 (precondition, not an edge) | Step 5.5 / Rule 21 (third party resolves) | The dependency is another in-flight strategy, not an epic in this decomposition; the third party (RHAISTRAT-9990) resolves it on its own schedule. Modeled as an AC gate (\"reports computed from the delivered metering data, no re-implementation of collection\") plus a risk entry, with tiered delivery so independent work is not blocked. |\n46\t\n47\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n48\t\n49\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n50\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n51\t| HLR-1 — per-project GPU-hour consumption reports over selectable time ranges | P0 | E002 (computation and API), E003 (administrator view) | Split along the backend/frontend architectural axis; both epics are P0. |\n52\t| HLR-2 — CSV export and stable API for finance tooling | P1 | E002 (bundled) — same report computation surface as HLR-1; CSV export and the finance API serialize the same computed report data over the same aggregation, so a separate epic would rebuild the aggregation surface and add an artificial serialization edge. | Bundled per Step 4 recording contract. |\n53\t| HLR-3 — current-month consumption against each project's Kueue quota | P1 | E001 (quota data side), E003 (view side — bundled with HLR-1: the quota comparison is a panel of the same administrator report view and the same consumption data; splitting it would fragment one UI surface and duplicate the view plumbing) | E001 is independently schedulable (Tier 1); the view-side bundling is recorded at the row. |\n54\t| HLR-4 — documentation of report semantics and export integration | P2 | E004 | docs-authoring epic, blocked by all Implementations (Rule 11), P1 per docs-authoring priority exception (strategy priority Major). |\n55\t\n56\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n57\t\n58\t## Health Warnings\n59\t\n60\t1. **Missing strategy sections.** The strategy has no Affected Components table (only a bare component list with no per-component change description), no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, and no non-functional requirements. Decomposition proceeded from the HLRs; acceptance criteria were derived from HLR-level requirements; owner teams were inferred from the architecture-context owning teams (Dashboard → RHOAI Dashboard; Distributed Workloads → Distributed Workloads). Flagged for refinement follow-up.\n61\t2. **External timing risk (strategy open question 1).** The metering data schema finalizes only when RHAISTRAT-9990 lands. This is not modeled as an Investigation: the resolution is owned by the other strategy (it cannot be resolved by work in this decomposition), and the reporting epics exist and deliver the same capability regardless of the schema's final shape — only the input contract and its timing vary. It is handled by tiered delivery (below), AC gates on E002, and risk entries on E002/E003. If RHAISTRAT-9990 slips indefinitely, Tier 2 (E002/E003) is deferred; Tier 1 (E001) retains independent value.\n62\t3. **Architecture tension on HLR-2.** The Dashboard architecture context (`architecture/rhoai-3.0/dashboard.md`) states the Dashboard's interfaces are \"Kubernetes API only; no private component APIs\" and that it \"owns no data plane\". HLR-2 requires \"a stable API for finance tooling\", and HLR-1 requires report computation — where the reporting API and computation are hosted (Dashboard backend vs a serving surface owned elsewhere) must be resolved by the implementing team against that constraint. E002's `architecture_claims` signal is scored -1 for this reason. Not an Investigation: it does not change which epics exist — the reporting/export capability is delivered either way; it changes where the code lands, which is an implementation-placement decision.\n63\t4. **Unspecified Distributed Workloads change.** The strategy lists Distributed Workloads as affected but describes no change for it. E001 was inferred from HLR-3's need for per-project Kueue quota data. If existing Kueue CRs/metrics already satisfy the need without code changes, E001's scope collapses to defining and documenting the consumption contract — this contingency is recorded in E001's scope; if it emerges that no Distributed Workloads change is warranted at all, E001 should be closed and E003's quota dependency re-pointed at the documented contract.\n64\t5. **E001 priority raised by inheritance.** E001 maps to HLR-3 (P1) but blocks E003 (P0), so priority inheritance makes it P0. This is rule-mandated, not a priority inversion; scheduling note: E001 is Tier 1 and can start immediately — it is not gated on the external metering dependency despite its P0 priority.\n65\t\n66\t## Tiered Delivery\n67\t\n68\tThe external dependency (RHAISTRAT-9990 metering data schema, uncertain timing) admits partial value without it:\n69\t\n70\t- **Tier 1 — independent work (starts immediately):** E001 (per-project Kueue quota data exposure). It consumes only Kueue-owned quota data, requires nothing from the metering strategy, and delivers the HLR-3 data side plus de-risking for the comparison view.\n71\t- **Tier 2 — dependency-gated work:** E002 and E003 (report computation, export, admin views) consume the metering data delivered by RHAISTRAT-9990. Gating is an acceptance-criterion gate plus risk on E002 (and transitively E003), never a DAG edge — the epics are ready to execute the moment the schema contract is available.\n72\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-836a73bf9feb0d1a","agent_id":"a3028f44fb113077f","session_id":"2d213be9-f60a-4255-a607-da491d2b37a9","uuid":"cff5f1fd-538e-418e-9495-0e4f0998141b","timestamp":"2026-10-26T17:28:02.826Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a233089bb8eb4c91","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9018 decomposition"}
